(a) **S.T.P. (B)** will be more effective. Sewage must first get **primary treatment**, the physical removal of particles, large and small, from the sewage through **filtration and sedimentation**. Only the primary effluent is then taken for **secondary (biological) treatment**. Plant (B) follows this order (sequential filtration, then biological treatment), whereas plant (A) puts the raw sewage, with all its floating debris, grit and solids, straight into biological treatment.

(b) In step (Z), the biological treatment, after the BOD of the sewage has been reduced significantly in the aeration tanks:
- the effluent is passed into a **settling tank**;
- there the **bacterial 'flocs' are allowed to sediment**; this sediment is called **activated sludge**;
- a **small part of the activated sludge is pumped back into the aeration tank** to serve as the **inoculum**;
- the **remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called anaerobic sludge digesters**.

